WEBVTT

00:01.824 --> 00:04.892
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.960 --> 00:08.295
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.362 --> 00:11.431
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.499 --> 00:14.401
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.469 --> 00:18.338
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.609 --> 00:21.460
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.544 --> 00:23.078
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.146 --> 00:24.446
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.514 --> 00:27.616
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:36.015 --> 00:38.050
(doorbell ringing)

00:39.326 --> 00:40.927
Hi. Package for
Brian Griffin.

00:40.995 --> 00:42.262
Oh, I can sign for that.

00:42.329 --> 00:44.197
Package, Bri.

00:44.265 --> 00:45.565
Huh. Wonder
what this is.

00:45.633 --> 00:48.033
It's my book.

00:48.101 --> 00:50.102
And there's a note
from my publisher.

00:50.170 --> 00:52.371
"We are returning
all of the unsold copies

00:52.438 --> 00:54.106
<i>of Faster Than
the Speed of Love."</i>

00:54.174 --> 00:55.674
You guys got a garage
or something?

00:55.742 --> 00:57.342
We got like 300 boxes
of these things.

00:57.410 --> 01:01.646
Wow, that's a lot of boxes!

01:04.750 --> 01:06.517
Oh, this is so weird.

01:06.585 --> 01:08.452
Look what they used
to pack your book in.

01:08.520 --> 01:10.154
Shredded-up pieces of your book.

01:10.222 --> 01:11.621
Okay, that's it, I'm finished.

01:11.689 --> 01:12.889
What are you
talking about?

01:12.957 --> 01:14.558
I'm-I'm finished.
I'm done. It's over.

01:14.625 --> 01:16.626
Clearly, I am not
meant to be a writer.

01:16.694 --> 01:19.262
I have tried and I have tried,
and nothing has worked out.

01:19.330 --> 01:22.098
I... put my heart out there, and
it keeps getting stomped on.

01:22.166 --> 01:24.067
Well, that's it, no more.

01:24.134 --> 01:25.701
I-I-I'm done writing.

01:25.769 --> 01:27.369
What? Writing's
the only thing

01:27.437 --> 01:28.771
that's giving your alcoholism
any credibility.

01:28.838 --> 01:30.439
Ooh, hey, you know, I just
thought of something,

01:30.507 --> 01:32.007
hold on a sec.

01:32.075 --> 01:34.577
I bought a vase last week
and it's still in the box,

01:34.644 --> 01:37.412
and I just want to check to see
what they used to pack it...

01:37.479 --> 01:38.913
Ah, it's your book!

01:42.451 --> 01:46.154
Oh, my God,
this gets me so angry!

01:46.205 --> 01:47.222
What? What is it?

01:47.289 --> 01:48.856
Look at this.

01:48.924 --> 01:51.391
<i>The number-one book on The New
York Times Bestseller list</i>

01:51.459 --> 01:53.961
<i>is Dream Your Way to
Spiritual Joy by Pauly Shore.</i>

01:54.028 --> 01:55.829
This is why nobody
bought my damn book.

01:55.897 --> 01:57.497
You know, this is
what they want.

01:57.565 --> 01:58.932
<i>The Secret.</i>

01:59.000 --> 02:00.701
<i>Chicken Soup for the Soul.
The Purpose Driven Life.</i>

02:00.768 --> 02:03.736
I-I tell ya, I could crap one
of those things out in a night.

02:03.804 --> 02:05.638
I'm gonna tell Mom
you said "crap."

02:05.706 --> 02:08.808
No, I mean it. I could do it in three
hours and idiots everywhere would buy it.

02:08.875 --> 02:11.844
Well, why don't you? Believe me, I
would love to, just to make a point,

02:11.912 --> 02:13.246
but I... I've
given up writing.

02:13.313 --> 02:15.180
Yes, but you cared about
what you wrote before.

02:15.248 --> 02:17.549
You don't care about this,
so what does it matter?

02:17.617 --> 02:19.117
Well, you do have a point.

02:19.185 --> 02:20.352
All right. Maybe I will.

02:20.419 --> 02:22.220
Oh, how fun. Can
I watch you write it?

02:22.288 --> 02:24.723
I don't know, Stewie,
I kind of like to write in solitude.

02:24.790 --> 02:27.558
No, no, I promise, I'll be as
quiet as a guy in a coma.

02:27.626 --> 02:28.892
<i>MAN:
Ah, this is great.</i>

02:28.960 --> 02:30.728
<i>I can finally be alone
with my thoughts.</i>

02:30.795 --> 02:33.931
<i>Wait, I've got it.
Predator versus Batman.</i>

02:33.999 --> 02:36.800
<i>Why has no one done this?
I need a pen, where's a pen?</i>

02:36.868 --> 02:39.403
<i>Oh, right, I can't move.
That's okay, I'll remember it.</i>

02:39.471 --> 02:41.237
<i>I mean, I can't forget a
million-dollar idea like that.</i>

02:41.305 --> 02:43.640
<i>It's even better than the one
yesterday about the guys who...</i>

02:43.707 --> 02:45.441
<i>Wait, I mean
the woman with the...</i>

02:45.509 --> 02:47.377
<i>What was it?
Was she a shapeshifter?</i>

02:47.444 --> 02:50.079
<i>Damn it, I just had it.
All right, calm down.</i>

02:50.147 --> 02:51.614
<i>At least you've still
got Batman and the...</i>

02:51.682 --> 02:53.348
<i>It was Batman Meets...</i>

02:53.416 --> 02:55.217
<i>Oh, you got to be kidding me,
I just had it.</i>

02:55.285 --> 02:57.386
<i>What was it, Batman Has a Son?</i>

02:57.453 --> 03:00.188
<i>No, no, you (bleep) idiot.
No wonder your wife shot you.</i>

03:05.161 --> 03:06.761
And... done.

03:06.828 --> 03:08.262
There it is, Stewie.

03:08.330 --> 03:09.997
Three hours, 27 minutes
and I've got myself

03:10.065 --> 03:11.532
one big steamin' pile of book.

03:11.600 --> 03:13.501
You want to hear it?
Oh, definitely.

03:13.568 --> 03:14.669
What are you calling it?

03:14.736 --> 03:15.870
<i>Wish It, Want It, Do It.</i>

03:15.937 --> 03:17.071
Love it.

03:17.139 --> 03:19.039
Thanks. Okay.
"Chapter One: Wish it.

03:19.106 --> 03:21.775
"What are all the things
you want most in the world?

03:21.842 --> 03:23.376
"Use the following
blank pages

03:23.444 --> 03:25.745
to write down all of your
dreams and desires."

03:25.813 --> 03:28.181
Ah. Brilliant. Make them do the work.
Yeah, why not?

03:28.249 --> 03:30.250
You know, i-i-if people want
crap, I'll give 'em crap.

03:30.318 --> 03:31.650
And, you know, I have a friend

03:31.718 --> 03:33.385
who might be able to help
get this published.

03:33.453 --> 03:34.653
Wait, you have a friend
in publishing

03:34.721 --> 03:36.188
and you never told me before?

03:36.256 --> 03:38.157
He's kind of a new friend.
Who is he?

03:38.224 --> 03:41.093
Yeah, I don't know if you want to
start digging around in this area.

03:41.161 --> 03:43.295
Let's just leave it at
he's a friend in publishing

03:43.363 --> 03:44.829
and he calls on Thursdays.

03:49.935 --> 03:53.204
Um, hey, Brian, when you're
done, can I make a pirate hat?

03:53.272 --> 03:54.439
(sighs) Here,
just take it.

03:56.374 --> 03:58.375
I look awesome.

03:58.443 --> 03:59.710
All right, I got to
go to a wedding.

03:59.777 --> 04:01.778
I'll see you later.

04:01.829 --> 04:03.513
Oh, my God, that is
fantastic news!

04:03.581 --> 04:05.716
I can't wait to tell him!

04:05.783 --> 04:07.351
I know, I told you
the book was good.

04:07.418 --> 04:10.152
Yeah, thank you
so much, Don.

04:10.220 --> 04:12.988
Um... red overalls,
a yellow shirt underneath--

04:13.056 --> 04:15.691
um, I-I-I don't really have
time to do this right now.

04:15.759 --> 04:16.959
Brian, they're publishing
your book.

04:17.027 --> 04:19.061
You're kidding.
I-I can't believe it.

04:19.129 --> 04:20.963
That's the worst thing
I've ever written.

04:21.031 --> 04:22.931
Not according
to Penguin Publishing.

04:22.998 --> 04:24.833
In fact, you're supposed
to call the chief editor.

04:24.900 --> 04:26.134
(phone ringing)

04:26.202 --> 04:27.535
Penguin Publishing.

04:27.603 --> 04:28.870
One moment, please.

04:28.938 --> 04:31.339
Sir, I have a Brian Griffin
on the line...

04:31.407 --> 04:33.274
Out. Get out!
Get out now!

04:40.236 --> 04:43.305
All right, everyone, I made
a very special breakfast

04:43.373 --> 04:45.240
to celebrate Brian's
book coming out.

04:45.268 --> 04:47.035
And... and...

04:47.102 --> 04:48.836
it's a special breakfast
for me, too, right?

04:48.904 --> 04:50.138
Why?

04:50.205 --> 04:52.573
Because... because it
is the anniversary

04:52.641 --> 04:55.576
of when I started
to eat vegetables.

04:55.644 --> 04:57.845
Author! Author!

04:57.913 --> 04:59.380
And Peter for
eating healthy.

04:59.447 --> 05:01.415
Oh, Lois, you didn't have
to go through all this fuss.

05:01.482 --> 05:02.916
What you got there?

05:02.984 --> 05:04.885
<i>Oh, this? It's just
a copy of Us magazine.</i>

05:04.952 --> 05:06.386
Can you believe it?

05:06.454 --> 05:08.622
<i>Wish it, Want it, Do it
got three stars.</i>

05:08.690 --> 05:10.057
How... How
hilarious is that?

05:10.124 --> 05:12.058
It's not hilarious;
its great, Brian.

05:12.125 --> 05:13.826
That's a huge accomplishment.

05:13.894 --> 05:16.028
Well, I helped get
the review in.

05:16.096 --> 05:17.730
<i>I called a friend
over at Us Weekly</i>

05:17.798 --> 05:20.199
who usually just covers people
with gross amounts of children,

05:20.267 --> 05:21.467
but now he's the book reviewer.

05:21.535 --> 05:23.269
So hats off to both of us.

05:23.336 --> 05:26.204
Well, n-nice going, Stewie.
You ought to be my publicist.

05:26.272 --> 05:28.373
You know, I-I don't want you
to be offering me this

05:28.441 --> 05:29.708
just because we're related.

05:29.775 --> 05:31.543
I do have references
you can call.

05:31.610 --> 05:33.211
You know, I-I spent a month

05:33.279 --> 05:35.113
as Melanie Griffith's
loose skin holder.

05:35.164 --> 05:35.997
Melanie! Melanie!
Over here.

05:36.082 --> 05:37.581
You look beautiful.

05:37.648 --> 05:41.885
Can't... hold... on...
much... longer... Whoa.

05:41.953 --> 05:43.353
(photographers gasping)

05:43.421 --> 05:45.522
(├а la Droopy Dog):
I should have stayed in bed.

05:48.460 --> 05:50.126
Yeah, we're walking in now.

05:50.194 --> 05:52.428
Make sure Tom Tucker knows
he has 20 minutes

05:52.496 --> 05:55.598
and he is to only talk about the book--
no personal questions, all right?

05:55.666 --> 05:58.101
Are you okay? You good? You...
You need anything? All right.

05:58.168 --> 06:00.503
All right, we're inside and there
is nobody here to greet us.

06:00.571 --> 06:03.238
We have no idea where we are or
where we're supposed to go.

06:03.306 --> 06:05.307
Oh wait, is that you
on the phone over there?

06:05.374 --> 06:07.309
Is this--? This is us.
Look over here.

06:07.376 --> 06:08.977
There we go.
He sees us. All right.

06:09.045 --> 06:10.112
Hi, thanks for coming.

06:10.179 --> 06:11.546
Yeah, hello,
we've been here awhile.

06:11.614 --> 06:13.281
Come on, I'll show
you the green room.

06:13.349 --> 06:16.083
And Mr. Griffin, I just have
to say, I loved your book.

06:16.151 --> 06:17.318
Oh, well, thanks.

06:17.369 --> 06:18.319
What's your name?
Sharhooz.

06:18.370 --> 06:19.487
Oh, my God.

06:19.554 --> 06:22.690
It'll just be a few minutes.

06:22.758 --> 06:25.159
Oh, uh, excuse me, there's
something on the wall.

06:27.028 --> 06:29.562
Now you have exactly 20
seconds to find us a room

06:29.630 --> 06:32.232
with a white sofa, a white
table, white hydrangeas

06:32.299 --> 06:33.516
and Fiji water!

06:34.835 --> 06:36.336
Geez, Stewie, there's
no need to--

06:36.403 --> 06:39.172
Ooh, I said no gray M & M's.
These are all gray.

06:39.240 --> 06:41.240
Don't worry, Brian.
I've got it all under control.

06:41.307 --> 06:42.875
You're a big canine star.

06:42.942 --> 06:44.576
They should be treating
you like Spuds MacKenzie.

06:44.644 --> 06:46.378
Man, I wonder
what he's doing now.

06:46.446 --> 06:48.580
(wheezing)

06:48.648 --> 06:50.516
Now, ladies,
approach him slowly.

06:50.583 --> 06:52.251
He can't really
see anymore.

06:52.318 --> 06:54.452
I've injected him
with the Viagra.

06:54.520 --> 06:56.154
Now, I'm gonna roll
him on his back,

06:56.221 --> 06:58.990
but you're pretty much gonna
have to do 100% of the work.

07:02.528 --> 07:05.229
We're here with Brian Griffin,
local author

07:05.297 --> 07:07.597
<i>of the new bestseller
Wish it, Want it, Do it.</i>

07:07.665 --> 07:09.432
Am I pronouncing
that correctly?

07:09.500 --> 07:10.867
Uh, yeah.

07:10.935 --> 07:13.570
<i>N-Now, what does that mean--
Wish It, Want It, Do It?</i>

07:13.638 --> 07:17.174
Uh, well, it... it basically just
refers to the steps necessary

07:17.241 --> 07:20.609
for identifying and achieving
your dreams and doing so

07:20.677 --> 07:23.445
in as effective and expeditious
a manner as possible.

07:23.513 --> 07:26.182
Wow. And it's
nonfiction, right?

07:26.249 --> 07:27.416
Y... uh... yes, it is.

07:27.484 --> 07:29.084
Now, w-which one is
that? I always forget.

07:29.152 --> 07:31.086
Is that the one that
really happened or not?

07:31.154 --> 07:33.054
Uh... yeah.
Okay, oh, so this...

07:33.122 --> 07:35.089
so now... so this
is... this is a true story.

07:35.157 --> 07:36.591
Well, it's not really
a narrative--

07:36.659 --> 07:38.660
Doesn't matter. I'm hooked.

07:38.727 --> 07:40.328
And we'll be right
back with sports.

07:40.396 --> 07:42.330
Okay, you're doing
great so far.

07:42.398 --> 07:43.998
Now I was just texting
with New York.

07:44.065 --> 07:45.399
We're doing a
whole thing there.

07:45.466 --> 07:47.401
I booked your plane ticket,
and I specifically asked

07:47.468 --> 07:49.102
that you not sit next
to an Asian.

07:49.170 --> 07:50.604
Stewie, that's racist.
That's terrible.

07:50.672 --> 07:53.273
I'm sorry, do you want me
to call back and change it?

07:53.341 --> 07:55.809
Well, it's done now.
Seems like a lot of extra work.

08:01.815 --> 08:03.049
Okay, there's a book signing

08:03.116 --> 08:05.301
at the Barnes and Noble
on Union Square at 5:00.

08:05.385 --> 08:07.620
And then at 7:00 you're
meeting with a reporter

08:07.671 --> 08:08.855
<i>from In Style magazine
at Pastis.</i>

08:08.922 --> 08:10.555
I just wanna warn you,
she's very heavy.

08:10.623 --> 08:12.224
So be prepared for that.

08:12.291 --> 08:14.259
Geez, how heavy?
I don't know, but apparently heavy enough

08:14.310 --> 08:16.094
that they felt they
needed to warn me.

08:16.145 --> 08:18.496
Oh, look at this, your book
is number three on Amazon.

08:18.564 --> 08:19.598
Number three?

08:19.665 --> 08:20.866
Well, who's number one?

08:20.933 --> 08:23.067
Oh, it's that new biography
on Michael Jackson.

08:23.134 --> 08:24.835
Oh, come on, another one?

08:24.903 --> 08:26.303
Unless it was written

08:26.371 --> 08:27.805
by a ten-year-old's
bottom, who cares?

08:27.873 --> 08:30.474
I... it doesn't say who
the author is, Brian,

08:30.542 --> 08:32.977
but I... I would guess that
it's probably not that.

08:33.044 --> 08:34.612
Well, Stewie,
I can't tell you

08:34.678 --> 08:36.012
how much I appreciate
all you're doing.

08:36.080 --> 08:37.947
I mean, I-I have been so
happy with most everything.

08:38.015 --> 08:39.649
Oh... y... Most?

08:39.717 --> 08:41.885
Oh, yeah... Oh, don't even, it's
so tiny, those other things,

08:41.952 --> 08:43.853
it's not even worth...
Oh, my God, you're amazing.

08:43.921 --> 08:45.455
I've been so thrilled
with how you're doing.

08:45.522 --> 08:46.522
Oh, great.

08:46.590 --> 08:47.856
Hi, can I help you?

08:47.924 --> 08:49.691
Yeah, uh... Checking in.
Brian Griffin.

08:49.759 --> 08:51.526
You... You should
usually do that.

08:51.594 --> 08:52.928
That... You should
check me in.

08:52.996 --> 08:55.097
That's like one of the
things I was talking about.

08:55.164 --> 08:58.300
I just have to tell you,
I loved your book, Mr. Griffin.

08:58.368 --> 09:01.369
Oh, thank you so much.
You made my day.

09:01.436 --> 09:02.603
So, you still in school?

09:02.671 --> 09:04.171
Graduating this year.

09:04.239 --> 09:06.374
Well, we'll have to
celebrate while I'm here.

09:06.441 --> 09:10.578
(giggles) Okay, well, I'll be
right back with your key.

09:10.646 --> 09:12.712
She's so pretty, isn't she?

09:12.780 --> 09:14.080
Yeah... you know.

09:14.148 --> 09:15.515
Do you think she liked me?

09:15.583 --> 09:16.883
I don't know.

09:16.951 --> 09:18.385
How... How weird would it be

09:18.452 --> 09:20.320
if she just showed up
at my room later?

09:20.388 --> 09:21.888
I... Pretty weird, I guess.

09:21.956 --> 09:24.157
Oh, my God, were you... were
you thinking of doing that?

09:24.225 --> 09:25.691
Sending her to my room?

09:25.759 --> 09:27.593
No... why?

09:27.661 --> 09:29.728
Why? Were you thinking
of me doing that?

09:29.796 --> 09:32.164
I don't even know what
you're saying anymore, man.

09:32.232 --> 09:34.233
Just... do... do whatever
you're thinking.

09:34.301 --> 09:36.068
Oh, man, crazy.

09:41.573 --> 09:44.642
Hi, um, I just want to say
that I can't tell you

09:44.710 --> 09:46.144
how much your book
has helped me.

09:46.211 --> 09:47.178
Uh-huh. What's your name?

09:47.246 --> 09:48.646
It's Jan.

09:48.714 --> 09:49.881
Okay, here you go, Dan.

09:49.949 --> 09:51.081
Y-Yeah, it's Jan.

09:51.149 --> 09:52.549
Back of the line. Go on.

09:52.600 --> 09:53.951
Come back around,
he'll try it again.

09:54.018 --> 09:56.153
Hi. Can you make
it out to Kelly?

09:56.220 --> 09:58.655
Well, hi there. I sure can.

09:58.723 --> 10:00.357
You know, I have to tell you,

10:00.425 --> 10:02.492
<i>Wish It, Want It, Do It
totally changed my life.</i>

10:02.560 --> 10:04.827
Well, you know, that's what I
set out to do with this book,

10:04.895 --> 10:08.831
so, you know, I gotta tell you,
that makes me feel pretty great.

10:08.899 --> 10:11.033
This is just a phone number.

10:11.101 --> 10:14.003
I know, I know,
my phone number.

10:14.071 --> 10:16.404
Oh.
Yeah.

10:16.472 --> 10:18.039
What do you think of that?

10:18.107 --> 10:20.675
Um, I'm not sure.

10:20.743 --> 10:21.977
Well, you know,
I'm sure for you.

10:22.044 --> 10:22.978
Oh.

10:23.045 --> 10:23.979
Yeah.

10:24.046 --> 10:24.980
I see.

10:25.047 --> 10:26.481
Yeah.

10:26.549 --> 10:28.116
I'm a little creeped out.

10:28.184 --> 10:31.301
Hey, congratulations,
somebody famous now hates you.

10:31.386 --> 10:34.188
Okay, so, that's enough
autographs, I think.

10:34.255 --> 10:36.523
Listen, I want to thank
you all for coming down.

10:36.591 --> 10:39.927
<i>And remember, Wish It,
Want It, Buy It. All right.</i>

10:39.995 --> 10:41.394
Brian, settle down.

10:41.462 --> 10:43.229
You're worse than that guy
from Penguin Publishing.

10:43.297 --> 10:45.398
You want to get a book
published, don't you?

10:45.466 --> 10:47.200
Well... yes.

10:47.267 --> 10:49.435
Well, if you want to be
in black and white,

10:49.503 --> 10:51.738
black and white's
gotta be in you.

11:00.480 --> 11:02.314
God, you know,
Stewie, I-I used

11:02.382 --> 11:04.816
to think that John Lennon
was kind of a jerk

11:04.884 --> 11:06.817
for saying The Beatles
were bigger than Jesus,

11:06.885 --> 11:08.819
but now, I mean, it's like,

11:08.887 --> 11:11.288
I'm not saying I am,
but I get it.

11:11.356 --> 11:13.324
You know, now that we have
two minutes

11:13.392 --> 11:14.692
to kinda let
the dust settle,

11:14.760 --> 11:17.294
I just wanna say
that I'm really proud of you.

11:17.362 --> 11:19.230
Hey, I'm proud of myself, man.

11:19.296 --> 11:20.830
I mean, I think
it's like... you know,

11:20.898 --> 11:23.266
I-I think everybody has
greatness in them,

11:23.334 --> 11:25.735
but it's-it's really about
having the courage

11:25.803 --> 11:27.737
to just kinda get inside
your own head

11:27.805 --> 11:30.407
and just kinda poke around in
there, you know, be, like,

11:30.474 --> 11:32.207
"Hey, oh, my gosh,
what's under here?

11:32.275 --> 11:33.842
<i>"Hey, what do you call yourself?</i>

11:33.910 --> 11:35.077
"Oh, Wisdom.

11:35.145 --> 11:36.111
"Oh, Profundity.

11:36.179 --> 11:38.580
"Oh, Truth.

11:38.648 --> 11:40.649
"Hey, let's, let's all just go
hang out together

11:40.717 --> 11:42.217
between the covers of a book."

11:42.285 --> 11:44.219
I love hearing
about your process.

11:44.287 --> 11:46.487
Hey, enough about me.
This was a great meal.

11:46.555 --> 11:48.423
Oh, good, good,
I'm glad you like it.

11:48.490 --> 11:49.924
They told me
everybody comes here.

11:51.226 --> 11:52.393
Hey, there's
Renee Zellweger.

11:52.461 --> 11:53.795
Hey, Renee,
how you doin'?

11:53.862 --> 11:55.563
Oh, hi, Brian!

12:02.036 --> 12:03.470
She seemed really nice.

12:03.538 --> 12:04.638
Get over here!

12:04.689 --> 12:05.839
Is, is everything okay?

12:05.907 --> 12:07.040
No, everything is
not okay.

12:07.108 --> 12:08.708
Can you figure out
what the problem is?

12:08.776 --> 12:10.776
I-I don't...
I-I honestly have no...

12:10.844 --> 12:12.178
Oh, God!

12:12.245 --> 12:15.081
How do you think I feel walking
out of the back room

12:15.148 --> 12:17.850
of a restaurant and seeing
Renee Zellweger eating

12:17.918 --> 12:19.585
in the front room
of that restaurant?

12:19.653 --> 12:20.653
I am mortified.

12:20.720 --> 12:21.987
Absolutely mortified!

12:22.055 --> 12:23.755
You should know better
than this!

12:23.823 --> 12:26.591
I-I told them who you were
when I made the reservation!

12:26.659 --> 12:29.327
Look, I have written
a best-selling phenomenon!

12:29.395 --> 12:31.796
I should be sitting in
the front damn room!!

12:31.864 --> 12:33.565
Okay, okay, I hear you, I hear you.

12:33.632 --> 12:36.600
And I just want you
to know that my only goal is

12:36.668 --> 12:38.869
to be able to help
you better.

12:38.937 --> 12:40.637
And you being
honest with me,

12:40.705 --> 12:42.106
well, that's helping
me do that.

12:42.173 --> 12:43.073
So thank you.

12:43.141 --> 12:44.274
Good.

12:46.744 --> 12:48.678
Oh, Brian, are you going
straight back to the hotel?

12:48.745 --> 12:50.680
'Cause I was gonna go back...

12:50.747 --> 12:52.748
You know what, I'll just...
I'll take a cab.

12:56.286 --> 12:57.553
Oh.

12:57.621 --> 12:59.489
Well, at least
it's not raining.

13:08.843 --> 13:10.778
Hey, Brian,
welcome home!

13:10.859 --> 13:12.092
How was your
book signing?

13:12.160 --> 13:13.459
Geez, Lois,
could I have,

13:13.527 --> 13:15.027
like, five minutes
to decompress, please?

13:15.095 --> 13:16.929
It was a really
horrible flight,

13:16.997 --> 13:21.934
<i>and I was sitting next to a
Japanese guy who had a cold.</i>

13:22.002 --> 13:24.670
<i>STEWIE: Dear God,
he's become a monster!
(kitchen cabinets banging)</i>

13:24.738 --> 13:26.972
BRIAN: Stewie, I got a flea
thing goin' on in here!

13:27.039 --> 13:28.039
Where the hell's
my Frontline?!

13:28.107 --> 13:30.709
(sighs)

13:30.777 --> 13:33.612
Oh, my God! Brian! Brian!

13:33.679 --> 13:34.813
Brian, look at this!

13:34.881 --> 13:37.182
<i>I got you on
Real Time with Bill Maher!</i>

13:37.250 --> 13:38.916
You're kidding?
That's awesome, Stewie!

13:38.984 --> 13:40.050
Great job!

13:40.118 --> 13:41.986
Oh, "great job,"
so you know that phrase.

13:42.053 --> 13:43.320
What's that?
What?

13:43.388 --> 13:45.089
What phrase do I know?

13:45.157 --> 13:46.624
Bill Maher, whoo!

13:51.362 --> 13:53.363
Okay, so the topic is
going to be,

13:53.430 --> 13:55.165
"Should God play
a role in politics?"

13:55.232 --> 13:57.834
Oh, and here's your heartworm
pill wrapped in bologna.

13:57.902 --> 13:59.536
Stewie, the point
of the bologna is

13:59.603 --> 14:01.471
that I don't know
the pill is there.

14:01.539 --> 14:03.706
Why am I standing
under an air vent?!

14:03.774 --> 14:05.073
Um... because...

14:05.141 --> 14:06.308
because that's where...

14:06.376 --> 14:07.442
Because that's where...

14:07.510 --> 14:09.111
I...
I...

14:09.179 --> 14:10.813
Because...
Because...

14:10.880 --> 14:11.980
Well...
Well...

14:12.048 --> 14:13.448
I can...
I can...

14:13.516 --> 14:14.583
Uh...
Uh..

14:14.651 --> 14:16.084
You...
Yeah...

14:16.152 --> 14:17.819
Uh... Yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah, uh.

14:17.886 --> 14:19.821
Okay, Mr. Griffin, we'll be
ready in five minutes.

14:19.888 --> 14:22.423
You'll be on the panel with Arianna
Huffington and Dana Gould.

14:22.491 --> 14:23.891
Whoa, whoa, wait, wait.

14:23.959 --> 14:25.226
My-my publicist told me
Christopher Hitchens

14:25.294 --> 14:26.594
would be on the panel.

14:26.662 --> 14:29.930
No, we told him Dana
and Arianna two hours ago.

14:29.997 --> 14:32.566
I did my prep research on
Christopher Hitchens.

14:32.633 --> 14:35.268
I am so... sorry.

14:35.336 --> 14:36.736
I am done with you.

14:36.804 --> 14:38.104
Do you hear me?! Done!

14:38.172 --> 14:39.406
Get out of here now!

14:39.473 --> 14:40.440
Brian, please.

14:40.508 --> 14:41.741
You're fired!

14:48.782 --> 14:51.116
Yes, but I think the more
important question

14:51.184 --> 14:52.918
that sometimes gets
ignored is

14:52.986 --> 14:55.086
what about the separation
of church and state?

14:55.154 --> 14:56.454
Well, Bill,
see, I think...

14:56.522 --> 14:58.156
Uh-uh-uh, actually, Dana,
if I may, if I may,

14:58.224 --> 15:00.091
I have some insight on this.
Bill, do you mind?

15:00.159 --> 15:01.459
Go, dog.

15:01.527 --> 15:02.460
(audience laughs)

15:02.528 --> 15:04.295
Well, it just so happens

15:04.363 --> 15:06.731
that separation of church and
state is something

15:06.799 --> 15:08.665
I'm actually gonna cover in
my next book,

15:08.733 --> 15:11.134
<i>God. Period. Dammit.</i>

15:11.202 --> 15:14.037
(audience laughs)

15:14.105 --> 15:15.405
What does that mean?

15:15.473 --> 15:17.808
Uh, well, if I can
expand on this.

15:17.876 --> 15:21.577
Um, for one thing,
"In God we trust" is on money.

15:21.645 --> 15:23.913
Now, what does that tell ya?

15:23.981 --> 15:27.183
Tells me Americans will get
all Christ-y

15:27.251 --> 15:29.385
about any sort
of stupid (bleep).

15:29.453 --> 15:31.153
So what?

15:31.221 --> 15:34.222
I think what's
actually alarming is the fact

15:34.290 --> 15:37.292
that all this kind
of pseudo spirituality is

15:37.360 --> 15:40.629
being peddled to the American
people in books that have,

15:40.696 --> 15:44.466
<i>really, less substance
than an issue of TV Guide.</i>

15:44.533 --> 15:45.966
That's a good argument.
It's a very good argument.

15:46.034 --> 15:47.368
I agree. I agree.

15:47.435 --> 15:51.739
<i>Really? Because I read
Wish It, Want It, Do It.</i>

15:51.790 --> 15:53.407
Oh, my God, I'm so embarrassed!

15:53.475 --> 15:54.942
Uh, gosh, next thing you know,

15:55.010 --> 15:56.644
both of you guys are
gonna tell me

15:56.711 --> 15:58.211
that it changed your lives.

15:58.279 --> 16:01.547
Uh, yeah, I,
I didn't read it.

16:01.615 --> 16:03.216
(audience laughs)

16:03.284 --> 16:06.252
Oh, hey, oh, so you're the guy.
(chuckles)

16:06.320 --> 16:07.887
It's-It's actually...
it's actually very popular.

16:07.955 --> 16:09.522
Well, it may be popular,

16:09.590 --> 16:12.457
but I, I have to say
I agree with Arianna.

16:12.525 --> 16:14.092
Uh, I think
it's actually quite harmful.

16:14.160 --> 16:16.361
Oh. Did you...?
When, when did you say that?

16:16.429 --> 16:18.063
I just did.

16:18.131 --> 16:19.598
(audience laughs)

16:19.666 --> 16:21.833
Oh, gosh, I'm sorry, maybe,
maybe 'cause it sounds

16:21.901 --> 16:23.567
like you're talking
with a mouthful of syrup,

16:23.635 --> 16:25.336
I just didn't get it.

16:25.404 --> 16:27.238
(audience laughs)

16:27.306 --> 16:28.940
Hey, (bleep) you, man.

16:29.007 --> 16:31.842
(audience laughs, applauds)

16:33.912 --> 16:37.647
Actually, it, it,
I mean, it seems

16:37.715 --> 16:41.618
that these sorts of books tend
to pander to the laziest kind

16:41.686 --> 16:44.321
of self-help within the
narrowest socioeconomic range.

16:44.388 --> 16:47.757
I mean, yeah, you u can wish it,
and you can do it,

16:47.825 --> 16:50.359
but only if you have
the educational advantages,

16:50.427 --> 16:52.761
the societal advantages
that, like, what,

16:52.829 --> 16:54.797
five per cent
of the country has.

16:54.864 --> 16:56.966
Well, yeah, you forgot "want
it," which is such a big part

16:57.033 --> 16:58.801
of the book. I mean, but, but
you know, then again,

16:58.868 --> 17:00.602
you just said you haven't read
it, so, you know...

17:00.670 --> 17:03.471
Actually, since I said
that, I did read it.

17:03.539 --> 17:05.106
(audience laughs)

17:05.174 --> 17:07.208
And that's another thing,
I have to say

17:07.276 --> 17:10.979
aren't "wish it" and "want it"
really the same thing?

17:11.046 --> 17:13.748
(audience laughs)
I mean, your book...

17:13.815 --> 17:15.749
basically makes three points,

17:15.817 --> 17:18.051
and two of them
are the same point.

17:18.119 --> 17:19.419
(audience laughs)

17:19.487 --> 17:21.888
Well, you know, I mean, it does
seem to be helping

17:21.956 --> 17:23.023
a lot of people, Bill...

17:23.091 --> 17:26.393
Well, "help" is a strong word.

17:26.461 --> 17:31.764
Um, how does this help people,
like, with cancer or in Darfur?

17:31.832 --> 17:34.200
Well, I mean,
it's not really for them.

17:34.267 --> 17:36.135
It's for, like,
if you want a car.

17:36.203 --> 17:37.403
(audience laughs)

17:41.674 --> 17:43.875
How-how does this help you get
a car?

17:43.943 --> 17:45.910
<i>Well, I mean,
it doesn't with that attitude.</i>

17:45.978 --> 17:49.047
I mean, you have...
(audience laughs)

17:49.115 --> 17:51.016
You have to do some
of the work yourself.

17:51.083 --> 17:52.516
That's why there are
50 blank pages.

17:52.584 --> 17:53.884
(audience laughs, applauds)

17:55.920 --> 17:58.622
That's why I keep it
by the phone.

17:58.690 --> 18:01.925
I think what this is
is simple exploitation

18:01.993 --> 18:04.594
of the American people who could
be using the money

18:04.661 --> 18:08.431
that they spend to buy this book
to actually buy something useful

18:08.499 --> 18:11.334
like legitimate healthcare
that they actually need.

18:11.402 --> 18:12.635
(applause)

18:14.938 --> 18:16.439
(clears throat)

18:16.507 --> 18:19.107
You know, what-what the hell's
your problem, Zsa Zsa?

18:19.175 --> 18:20.242
(audience laughs)

18:20.309 --> 18:22.544
What is your problem, Snoopy?

18:22.612 --> 18:25.714
(audience laughs)

18:25.782 --> 18:29.451
Look, what it comes down to is that these
false promises of, like, a quick fix

18:29.519 --> 18:32.053
to any and every problem
that you would ever have,

18:32.120 --> 18:34.688
what books like these suggest is
actually damaging

18:34.756 --> 18:37.525
to a large, gullible segment
of the population.

18:37.592 --> 18:39.493
Oh, yeah, aren't you
a sitcom writer?

18:39.561 --> 18:42.163
I'm an author. I have
contributed to the zeitgeist.

18:42.230 --> 18:43.830
Do you even know
what "zeitgeist" means?

18:43.898 --> 18:46.933
If I didn't know what it meant,
I wouldn't use it, Dana--

18:47.001 --> 18:50.670
which... which is a girl's
name-- and I'm...

18:50.738 --> 18:52.405
(audience laughs)
And you know,

18:52.473 --> 18:54.574
I-I'm not gonna
give you, I'm not gonna give you

18:54.642 --> 18:56.742
the definition right now just
for your satisfaction.

18:56.810 --> 18:59.445
You know, Brian, I don't think
you're in a position, really,

18:59.512 --> 19:01.080
here to pull rank on anybody.

19:01.147 --> 19:03.649
I mean, yes, Dana has made
a career in comedy.

19:03.716 --> 19:05.417
I don't think
he's ever done anything

19:05.485 --> 19:06.819
as laughable as this book.

19:06.886 --> 19:08.119
Look, all right, well...

19:08.187 --> 19:11.222
What do you want?
I wrote it in a day.

19:11.290 --> 19:14.192
(audience groans)
Listen, Bill, I am

19:14.259 --> 19:15.760
such a big fan of yours.

19:15.828 --> 19:17.762
I think you're totally getting
the wrong impression of me.

19:17.830 --> 19:19.597
I-I was just trying to write
something that would sell.

19:19.665 --> 19:20.997
I-I think it's crap, too.

19:21.065 --> 19:24.868
Wow. If I had even one shred

19:24.936 --> 19:29.239
of respect for you before this,
it's gone now.

19:29.307 --> 19:31.808
I mean, if you're gonna dump on
people,

19:31.876 --> 19:36.712
the kind of steaming, stinking,
smelly dump

19:36.780 --> 19:38.614
that your kind traffics in...

19:38.682 --> 19:40.850
(audience laughs)

19:40.917 --> 19:43.519
...at least stand by your dump.

19:43.587 --> 19:45.854
(audience laughs)

19:48.057 --> 19:50.158
O-okay, look,
I'm happy to do that.

19:50.226 --> 19:51.826
I-I-I'm just, I'm just try...

19:51.894 --> 19:53.495
Look, what I was saying...

19:53.562 --> 19:55.797
Look, just tell me
what you want me to say!

19:55.865 --> 19:57.899
Bill, he's urinating!

19:57.967 --> 19:58.932
(bleep)

19:59.000 --> 20:00.033
That's it!

20:00.101 --> 20:01.168
Look, Bill, can I just...?
Outside.

20:01.236 --> 20:03.036
No, no, no, outside!
Outside now!

20:03.104 --> 20:04.805
(barking)
Outside!

20:04.873 --> 20:07.007
You get outside!

20:12.946 --> 20:14.847
Uh, knock-knock.

20:14.915 --> 20:16.149
Hey, Stewie.

20:16.216 --> 20:17.617
<i>What do you want?</i>

20:17.684 --> 20:19.952
I just wanna talk to you about
everything that happened.

20:20.020 --> 20:23.523
Um, you know,
you were the only person

20:23.590 --> 20:25.957
who believed in me
when I was down.

20:26.025 --> 20:28.660
This all happened so fast,
and I lost sight of who I was.

20:28.728 --> 20:30.162
I mean, you know, sure,

20:30.229 --> 20:32.364
you were in
a little over your head, but...

20:32.431 --> 20:33.598
Whoa, whoa, whoa,
whoa, whoa.

20:33.666 --> 20:35.267
<i>I was in
over my head?</i>

20:35.334 --> 20:37.468
Yeah, but, Stewie, this isn't about
all the things you did wrong.

20:37.536 --> 20:39.470
It's about me apologizing.

20:39.538 --> 20:40.638
Okay, then apologize.

20:40.705 --> 20:42.706
I just did.
No, you didn't.

20:42.774 --> 20:44.108
You just said it's
about me apologizing.

20:44.176 --> 20:45.209
That's not actually
apologizing.

20:45.277 --> 20:46.744
All right, Stewie,
I'm sorry

20:46.811 --> 20:49.046
that I made you uncomfortable
and put you in a situation

20:49.113 --> 20:50.480
that you clearly
couldn't handle.

20:50.548 --> 20:51.981
Okay, okay,
there it is again.

20:52.049 --> 20:53.283
What the hell?
Stop with that!

20:53.350 --> 20:54.284
Y-you're right.
You're right.

20:54.351 --> 20:55.685
This is,
this is about healing.

20:55.753 --> 20:57.520
This, this is not about
how many things

20:57.588 --> 20:58.955
you messed up
along the way.

20:59.023 --> 21:00.990
It's about how badly
I reacted to them.

21:01.058 --> 21:03.792
So, I'm sorry
about how badly I reacted

21:03.860 --> 21:05.561
to your many errors.

21:05.628 --> 21:07.996
That's as good as it's
gonna get, isn't it?

21:08.064 --> 21:10.032
Pretty much.
You can't write.

21:10.033 --> 22:11.033
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru